<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abu Bakr Atiku</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1842">1842</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Abubakar Atiku</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abu Bakr Atiku was the second <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto</a>, ruling from 1837 to 1842. A son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usman-dan-fodio">Usman dan Fodio</a>, the founder of the caliphate, he succeeded his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-bello">Muhammad Bello</a> and was known for his strict religious policy. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umaru-nagwamatse">Umaru Nagwamatse</a> later founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kontagora">Kontagora</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a>, and another son, Abubakar II Atiku na Raba, also became caliph.</p></div>
